      "html": "<p>Five tools a Python engineer meets at the edges of the language, each a BREADTH note with one trap worth knowing. Each one applies a mechanism from an earlier chapter; read each arrow as a sentence: <em>NumPy vectorization</em> <strong>built on</strong> <em>why Python is slow</em>.</p>\n<figure><div class=\"mermaid\">flowchart TD\n    SLOW[\"🐢 58 · why Python is slow\"]\n    TEST[\"✅ 67 · testing &amp; tooling\"]\n    LOCK[\"🧪 65 · venvs &amp; resolution\"]\n    N83[\"🧮 83 · NumPy vectorization\"]\n    N84[\"🐼 84 · pandas memory\"]\n    N85[\"🗄️ 85 · SQLAlchemy vs raw SQL\"]\n    N86[\"🐒 86 · monkey patching\"]\n    N87[\"🐳 87 · Docker for Python\"]\n    N83 -.-&gt;|\"built on\"| SLOW\n    N84 --&gt;|\"built on\"| N83\n    N86 -.-&gt;|\"same mechanism as\"| TEST\n    N87 -.-&gt;|\"built on\"| LOCK\n    classDef br fill:#FCD34D,stroke:#F59E0B,color:#000\n    classDef in fill:#D1D5DB,stroke:#9CA3AF,color:#000\n    class N83,N84,N85,N86,N87 br; class SLOW,TEST,LOCK in\n</div></figure>\n<p>Yellow = BREADTH, grey = another chapter (dashed edges). 85 stands alone here; its neighbour is <a href=\"/python/connection-pooling-async\">connection pooling</a> (N+1 queries exhaust any pool).</p>\n<div class=\"flow-chain\"><span class=\"flow-label\"><strong>Reading order:</strong></span><span class=\"flow-step\"><a href=\"/python/numpy-vectorization\">83 NumPy vectorization</a></span><span class=\"flow-arrow\"></span><span class=\"flow-step\"><a href=\"/python/pandas-memory-efficiency\">84 pandas memory</a></span><span class=\"flow-arrow\"></span><span class=\"flow-step\"><a href=\"/python/sqlalchemy-vs-raw-sql\">85 SQLAlchemy vs raw SQL</a></span><span class=\"flow-arrow\"></span><span class=\"flow-step\"><a href=\"/python/monkey-patching\">86 monkey patching</a></span><span class=\"flow-arrow\"></span><span class=\"flow-step\"><a href=\"/python/docker-for-python\">87 Docker for Python</a></span></div>\n<p>No decision tree here: these notes are separate tools, not alternatives for one choice.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The one big idea:</strong> each tool hides a cost you only see when you measure it: interpreter dispatch (83), dtype width (84), implicit queries (85), invisible global state (86), layer order (87).</li>\n<li><strong>The two most-confused pairs:</strong> <em>vectorized vs <code>.apply()</code></em> (83: C loop vs a Python callable per row) · <em>patch where defined vs where used</em> (86: only the second takes effect after a <code>from</code>-import).</li>\n<li><strong>Ten minutes before an interview:</strong> the measured numbers: 127× for vectorizing, 67 MB → 9 MB for dtypes, 101 → 2 queries for <code>selectinload</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Cross-chapter:</strong> <a href=\"/python/why-python-is-slow\">why Python is slow</a> (the cost model behind 83–84) · <a href=\"/python/testing-quality-tooling\">testing</a> (patch-where-used, measured) · <a href=\"/python/virtual-environments-dependency-resolution\">venvs &amp; locks</a> (what the Docker build installs from).</li>\n</ul>"
    },
