      "html": "<h5><svg class=\"md-icon\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" width=\"1.15em\" height=\"1.15em\" fill=\"currentColor\" aria-hidden=\"true\" style=\"vertical-align:-0.2em;flex-shrink:0\"><path d=\"M21 5c-1.11-.35-2.33-.5-3.5-.5-1.95 0-4.05.4-5.5 1.5-1.45-1.1-3.55-1.5-5.5-1.5S2.45 4.9 1 6v14.65c0 .25.25.5.5.5.1 0 .15-.05.25-.05C3.1 20.45 5.05 20 6.5 20c1.95 0 4.05.4 5.5 1.5 1.35-.85 3.8-1.5 5.5-1.5 1.65 0 3.35.3 4.75 1.05.1.05.15.05.25.05.25 0 .5-.25.5-.5V6c-.6-.45-1.25-.75-2-1m0 13.5c-1.1-.35-2.3-.5-3.5-.5-1.7 0-4.15.65-5.5 1.5V8c1.35-.85 3.8-1.5 5.5-1.5 1.2 0 2.4.15 3.5.5z\"></path><path d=\"M17.5 10.5c.88 0 1.73.09 2.5.26V9.24c-.79-.15-1.64-.24-2.5-.24-1.7 0-3.24.29-4.5.83v1.66c1.13-.64 2.7-.99 4.5-.99M13 12.49v1.66c1.13-.64 2.7-.99 4.5-.99.88 0 1.73.09 2.5.26V11.9c-.79-.15-1.64-.24-2.5-.24-1.7 0-3.24.3-4.5.83m4.5 1.84c-1.7 0-3.24.29-4.5.83v1.66c1.13-.64 2.7-.99 4.5-.99.88 0 1.73.09 2.5.26v-1.52c-.79-.16-1.64-.24-2.5-.24\"></path></svg> References</h5>\n<p><svg class=\"md-icon\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" width=\"1.15em\" height=\"1.15em\" fill=\"currentColor\" aria-hidden=\"true\" style=\"vertical-align:-0.2em;flex-shrink:0\"><path d=\"M8 16h8v2H8zm0-4h8v2H8zm6-10H6c-1.1 0-2 .9-2 2v16c0 1.1.89 2 1.99 2H18c1.1 0 2-.9 2-2V8zm4 18H6V4h7v5h5z\"></path></svg> <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/user_guide/scale.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">pandas user guide: scaling to large datasets</a> · <svg class=\"md-icon\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" width=\"1.15em\" height=\"1.15em\" fill=\"currentColor\" aria-hidden=\"true\" style=\"vertical-align:-0.2em;flex-shrink:0\"><path d=\"M8 16h8v2H8zm0-4h8v2H8zm6-10H6c-1.1 0-2 .9-2 2v16c0 1.1.89 2 1.99 2H18c1.1 0 2-.9 2-2V8zm4 18H6V4h7v5h5z\"></path></svg> <a href=\"https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/user_guide/copy_on_write.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\">pandas: Copy-on-Write</a></p>\n<h3 id=\"tldr\"><svg class=\"md-icon\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" width=\"1.15em\" height=\"1.15em\" fill=\"currentColor\" aria-hidden=\"true\" style=\"vertical-align:-0.2em;flex-shrink:0\"><path d=\"M11 21h-1l1-7H7.5c-.88 0-.33-.75-.31-.78C8.48 10.94 10.42 7.54 13.01 3h1l-1 7h3.51c.4 0 .62.19.4.66C12.97 17.55 11 21 11 21\"></path></svg> TL;DR</h3>\n<p>Pandas memory is dtype-driven: downcast numerics, make low-cardinality strings <code>category</code>, declare <code>dtype=</code> at read time, stream chunks when it won't fit.\nSince pandas 3.0, Copy-on-Write is always on: chained assignment never modifies the original.</p>\n<h3 id=\"recall\"><svg class=\"md-icon\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" width=\"1.15em\" height=\"1.15em\" fill=\"currentColor\" aria-hidden=\"true\" style=\"vertical-align:-0.2em;flex-shrink:0\"><path d=\"m19.07 4.93-1.41 1.41C19.1 7.79 20 9.79 20 12c0 4.42-3.58 8-8 8s-8-3.58-8-8c0-4.08 3.05-7.44 7-7.93v2.02C8.16 6.57 6 9.03 6 12c0 3.31 2.69 6 6 6s6-2.69 6-6c0-1.66-.67-3.16-1.76-4.24l-1.41 1.41C15.55 9.9 16 10.9 16 12c0 2.21-1.79 4-4 4s-4-1.79-4-4c0-1.86 1.28-3.41 3-3.86v2.14c-.6.35-1 .98-1 1.72 0 1.1.9 2 2 2s2-.9 2-2c0-.74-.4-1.38-1-1.72V2h-1C6.48 2 2 6.48 2 12s4.48 10 10 10 10-4.48 10-10c0-2.76-1.12-5.26-2.93-7.07\"></path></svg> Recall</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Prompt</th>\n<th>Answer</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody><tr>\n<td><strong>Biggest quick memory win?</strong></td>\n<td>Downcast numerics (<code>int64</code> → <code>uint32</code>, <code>float64</code> → <code>float32</code>) and turn low-cardinality strings into <code>category</code>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong><code>df[mask][\"b\"] = 99</code> in pandas 3?</strong></td>\n<td>Never sticks: Copy-on-Write makes the intermediate a copy, with a <code>ChainedAssignmentError</code> warning. Use <code>df.loc[mask, \"b\"] = 99</code>.</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>A file bigger than RAM?</strong></td>\n<td><code>read_csv(chunksize=…)</code>, reducing per chunk.</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3 id=\"the-idea\"><svg class=\"md-icon\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" width=\"1.15em\" height=\"1.15em\" fill=\"currentColor\" aria-hidden=\"true\" style=\"vertical-align:-0.2em;flex-shrink:0\"><path d=\"M9 21c0 .55.45 1 1 1h4c.55 0 1-.45 1-1v-1H9zm3-19C8.14 2 5 5.14 5 9c0 2.38 1.19 4.47 3 5.74V17c0 .55.45 1 1 1h6c.55 0 1-.45 1-1v-2.26c1.81-1.27 3-3.36 3-5.74 0-3.86-3.14-7-7-7m2.85 11.1-.85.6V16h-4v-2.3l-.85-.6C7.8 12.16 7 10.63 7 9c0-2.76 2.24-5 5-5s5 2.24 5 5c0 1.63-.8 3.16-2.15 4.1\"></path></svg> The idea</h3>\n<p>Columns are arrays, so memory follows dtype. A 1M-row frame of <code>int64</code> user IDs, four country strings and <code>float64</code> amounts took 67.0 MB (pandas 3.0.6, <code>memory_usage(deep=True)</code>); with <code>uint32</code>, <code>category</code> and <code>float32</code> it took 9.0 MB. A <code>category</code> stores integer codes plus one lookup table. Declare dtypes in <code>read_csv</code> so the wasteful version is never built. Pandas 3 also settled copy-vs-view: Copy-on-Write is always enabled, so <code>d[d[\"a\"] &gt; 1][\"b\"] = 99</code> left <code>b</code> as <code>[1, 2, 3]</code> and raised <code>ChainedAssignmentError</code>. Past one machine's memory, leave pandas for Polars or DuckDB.</p>\n<h3 id=\"code\"><svg class=\"md-icon\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\" width=\"1.15em\" height=\"1.15em\" fill=\"currentColor\" aria-hidden=\"true\" style=\"vertical-align:-0.2em;flex-shrink:0\"><path d=\"M9.4 16.6 4.8 12l4.6-4.6L8 6l-6 6 6 6zm5.2 0 4.6-4.6-4.6-4.6L16 6l6 6-6 6z\"></path></svg> Code</h3>\n<pre><code class=\"language-python\">import pandas as pd\ndf = pd.read_csv(\"events.csv\", dtype={\"user_id\": \"uint32\", \"country\": \"category\"})\ndf[\"amount\"] = pd.to_numeric(df[\"amount\"], downcast=\"float\")    # float64 → float32\nprint(df.memory_usage(deep=True))           # deep: count string bytes\ndf.loc[df[\"amount\"] &gt; 100, \"flag\"] = True   # one indexing step: the CoW-safe way to write\ntotal = sum(chunk[\"amount\"].sum()           # out-of-core: one chunk resident at a time\n            for chunk in pd.read_csv(\"events.csv\", chunksize=100_000))\n</code></pre>"
    },
